        <h2>What Is a Liver Function Test (LFT)?</h2>
        <p>A Liver Function Test (LFT) is a group of blood tests that assess the health and function of your liver. The liver is one of the largest and most vital organs — it detoxifies blood, produces bile for digestion, stores energy, and synthesizes proteins essential for blood clotting.</p>
        <p>LFT is commonly part of routine health checkups in India. It is specifically ordered when there is suspected liver disease, fatty liver, jaundice, hepatitis, or when monitoring medications that can affect the liver.</p>

        <h2>Key LFT Parameters Explained</h2>

        <h3>1. SGPT / ALT (Serum Glutamate Pyruvate Transaminase / Alanine Aminotransferase)</h3>
        <p>SGPT (also known as ALT) is an enzyme found primarily in the liver. When liver cells are damaged or inflamed, SGPT leaks into the bloodstream. <strong>SGPT is the most liver-specific enzyme</strong> and is the primary marker doctors look at.</p>
        <div class="bg-canvas-soft border border-hairline rounded-lg p-4 my-4">
          <p class="font-semibold text-primary mb-2">Normal SGPT (ALT) Range:</p>
          <ul class="text-sm space-y-1 m-0">
            <li>Men: <strong>7 – 56 U/L</strong> (many Indian labs use &lt; 40 U/L as cutoff)</li>
            <li>Women: <strong>7 – 45 U/L</strong> (many Indian labs use &lt; 35 U/L as cutoff)</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <p><strong>Mildly elevated SGPT (40–100 U/L):</strong> Very common in India. Most frequent causes are Non-Alcoholic Fatty Liver Disease (NAFLD), obesity, metabolic syndrome, or certain medications.</p>
        <p><strong>Moderately elevated (100–300 U/L):</strong> May indicate active hepatitis, drug-induced liver injury, or alcoholic liver disease.</p>
        <p><strong>Highly elevated (&gt;300 U/L):</strong> Suggests significant liver damage — viral hepatitis (A, B, E), drug toxicity (paracetamol overdose), or other acute liver conditions.</p>

        <h3>2. SGOT / AST (Serum Glutamate Oxaloacetate Transaminase / Aspartate Aminotransferase)</h3>
        <p>SGOT (also known as AST) is found in the liver, heart, and muscles. It is less liver-specific than SGPT because it can also be elevated from muscle injury or heart conditions.</p>
        <div class="bg-canvas-soft border border-hairline rounded-lg p-4 my-4">
          <p class="font-semibold text-primary mb-2">Normal SGOT (AST) Range:</p>
          <ul class="text-sm space-y-1 m-0">
            <li>Adults: <strong>8 – 48 U/L</strong> (many Indian labs use &lt; 40 U/L)</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <p><strong>The SGOT:SGPT Ratio (De Ritis Ratio):</strong> If SGOT &gt; SGPT (ratio &gt; 2), it may suggest alcoholic liver disease. If SGPT &gt; SGOT, it more commonly suggests non-alcoholic fatty liver.</p>

        <h3>3. Bilirubin (Total and Direct)</h3>
        <p>Bilirubin is a yellow pigment produced when old red blood cells are broken down. The liver processes bilirubin and excretes it in bile. High bilirubin causes jaundice (yellowing of skin and eyes).</p>
        <div class="bg-canvas-soft border border-hairline rounded-lg p-4 my-4">
          <p class="font-semibold text-primary mb-2">Normal Bilirubin Ranges:</p>
          <ul class="text-sm space-y-1 m-0">
            <li>Total Bilirubin: <strong>0.1 – 1.2 mg/dL</strong></li>
            <li>Direct (Conjugated) Bilirubin: <strong>0.0 – 0.3 mg/dL</strong></li>
            <li>Indirect Bilirubin: Total minus Direct</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>High Indirect Bilirubin:</strong> Suggests increased red blood cell breakdown (hemolysis) or Gilbert's syndrome (a common, harmless genetic condition affecting ~5% of the Indian population)</li>
          <li><strong>High Direct Bilirubin:</strong> Suggests a liver or bile duct problem (obstruction, hepatitis, or cirrhosis)</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>4. Alkaline Phosphatase (ALP)</h3>
        <p>ALP is an enzyme found in the liver, bones, and bile ducts. Elevated ALP combined with elevated GGT specifically points to bile duct or liver problems (rather than bone).</p>
        <div class="bg-canvas-soft border border-hairline rounded-lg p-4 my-4">
          <ul class="text-sm space-y-1 m-0">
            <li>Adults: <strong>44 – 147 U/L</strong></li>
            <li>Children/Adolescents: Higher values are normal (growing bones produce ALP)</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <h3>5. GGT (Gamma-Glutamyl Transferase)</h3>
        <p>GGT is an enzyme sensitive to liver and bile duct damage. It is also elevated by alcohol consumption and certain medications.</p>
        <div class="bg-canvas-soft border border-hairline rounded-lg p-4 my-4">
          <ul class="text-sm space-y-1 m-0">
            <li>Men: <strong>8 – 65 U/L</strong></li>
            <li>Women: <strong>5 – 36 U/L</strong></li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <h3>6. Total Protein and Albumin</h3>
        <p>The liver produces albumin and other proteins. Low albumin can indicate chronic liver disease.</p>
        <div class="bg-canvas-soft border border-hairline rounded-lg p-4 my-4">
          <ul class="text-sm space-y-1 m-0">
            <li>Total Protein: <strong>6.0 – 8.3 g/dL</strong></li>
            <li>Albumin: <strong>3.5 – 5.5 g/dL</strong></li>
            <li>A/G Ratio (Albumin/Globulin): <strong>1.1 – 2.5</strong></li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <h2>NAFLD: India's Silent Liver Epidemic</h2>
        <p>Non-Alcoholic Fatty Liver Disease (NAFLD) is the most common cause of mildly elevated SGPT in India. It affects an estimated 30–40% of urban Indians and is strongly associated with:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Obesity and central (belly) fat</li>
          <li>Type 2 Diabetes / Insulin Resistance</li>
          <li>High triglycerides and low HDL cholesterol</li>
          <li>Sedentary lifestyle</li>
          <li>Excess refined carbohydrates in diet</li>
        </ul>
        <p><strong>The good news:</strong> NAFLD is reversible with lifestyle changes. Losing 7–10% of body weight can normalize liver enzymes and reverse fatty liver in most patients.</p>

        <h2>Indian Diet Tips for Liver Health</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>✅ <strong>Green tea:</strong> Contains catechins with hepatoprotective properties</li>
          <li>✅ <strong>Turmeric (haldi):</strong> Curcumin has anti-inflammatory and liver-protective effects</li>
          <li>✅ <strong>Amla (Indian gooseberry):</strong> Rich in Vitamin C and antioxidants</li>
          <li>✅ <strong>Whole grains:</strong> Replace maida with whole wheat, bajra, jowar, oats</li>
          <li>✅ <strong>Coffee:</strong> 2–3 cups daily is associated with reduced liver fibrosis</li>
          <li>❌ <strong>Limit:</strong> Alcohol (obvious), excessive sugar and refined flour, deep-fried snacks, processed meats</li>
          <li>❌ <strong>Avoid:</strong> Self-medicating with paracetamol/acetaminophen in large doses (max 4g/day; less with alcohol use)</li>
        </ul>

